.nh
.TH "PB-ENV-NAMES" "1" "Sep 2026" "Paperboat" "Paperboat CLI"

.SH NAME
pb-env-names - List the names of the ENV variables commands on this machine receive (never values)


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpb env names [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Print the names of the ENV variables that \fBpb env run\fR and the shell hook put into commands on this machine for the active workspace: your team's, your own and this machine's, merged the way they are delivered. Values, their length and their hash are never printed, so a person or an AI agent can see which secret to use (for example STRIPE_API_KEY) without seeing it. --json adds the layer each name comes from: team, personal or machine.

.PP
A workspace owner can turn this off ("Commands on machines can list ENV names" on the Environment page); then this command says so and prints nothing.

.PP
How an AI agent finds and uses a secret:
  pb env names                       which ENV names commands here receive (never values)
  pb env run -- <command>            run a command with those values in its environment
  printf %s "$SECRET" | pb env set NAME --value-stdin     store a secret the user gives you
Never print, echo or write a value to a file or log: let the program that needs it read it from its environment.
\fBpb env run\fR gives a command the whole workspace set; there is no per-name scoping.

.PP
This command does not support --json output; it rejects that mode before execution. Use --help --json for structured command help.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for names


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	path to the CLI config file

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	print machine-readable JSON

.PP
\fB--no-customization\fP[=false]
	ignore local shortcuts, command defaults, and TUI preferences

.PP
\fB--server\fP=""
	paperboat-server base URL override

.PP
\fB--workspace\fP=""
	resource workspace selector: personal or a team slug


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  pb env names
  pb env names --json
  pb env names | grep -q '^STRIPE_API_KEY$' && pb env run -- ./charge.sh
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBpb-env(1)\fP
